// expect-error: O001
// Z3 corpus #8: axis-1 inexpressibility evidence — rate limits.
//
// Step 9 (first iteration under the axes-1/3/5 supremum prompt) attempted
// to encode a rate-limit pattern using only existing primitives (actors,
// cap types, linear move, `.split()`). The attempt FAILS at the language
// surface because `cap.split(amount)` CONSUMES the parent cap rather
// than preserving a remainder.
//
// The pattern below tries to spawn two workers each with one shard of
// `fuel`. The second `fuel.split(1)` is rejected by the ownership
// checker (O001) because the first split moved `fuel`. Generalizing:
// any pattern that wants N sequential draws from a single parent
// budget hits this wall after the first draw.
//
// Why this matters for the supremum:
//   * Rate limits, quotas, N-of-M draws, and pool-style allocation
//     are documented motivating axis-1 patterns. None are expressible
//     today.
//   * The user is forced to either (a) hard-code N=1 (single use), or
//     (b) drop into actor message-passing that carries data instead
//     of caps. Option (b) is the escape hatch the supremum forbids:
//     the receiver gets data with no cap-typed safety guarantee.
//
// Smallest missing primitive (acceptance criteria for the NEXT axis-1
// iteration):
//
//   Option A: `cap.draw(amount) -> Cap` that returns a shard AND
//             leaves the parent alive bound to a remainder of
//             `parent.amount - amount`. The parent's identity
//             persists; only its quantity decreases. Linear semantics
//             preserved because the parent is still a single cap
//             with a single identity.
//
//   Option B: `cap.fork(n) -> [Cap; N]` that splits into a fixed
//             compile-time `N` shards at once. Consumes parent.
//             Returns an array binding. Less flexible than (A) but
//             a single new primitive at the AIR level.
//
//   Option C: tuple-returning split: `let (shard, fuel) = fuel.split(1)`
//             where `fuel` on the RHS is consumed and on the LHS is
//             rebound to the remainder. Requires shadowing-by-let
//             semantics that may not exist today.
//
// Recommendation (subject to next iteration's design review): Option A
// is simplest because it adds ONE new AIR stmt (`AirStmt::CapDraw`)
// with a tight Z3 conservation rule (parent.amount -= amount AND
// child.amount = amount), and reuses the existing CapSplit Phase-1
// legitimacy machinery. Options B and C require either array-of-cap
// types or new binding forms — bigger surface.
//
// Next axis-1 iteration's acceptance criteria:
//   1. A new fixture `09_rate_limit_via_draw.sigil` compiles cleanly
//      and demonstrates two sequential `fuel.draw(1)` operations.
//   2. This fixture (#08) remains rejected — `cap.split(1)` keeps its
//      consuming semantics; only `cap.draw(1)` gets the new behavior.
//   3. Z3 conservation: assert `parent_remaining + child_amount ==
//      parent_initial` per Draw site.
//   4. tokei runtime LOC unchanged (compile-time addition only).
//
// MUTATION CONTRACT: deleting MUTATION_SITE removes the second spawn
// entirely. `fuel` is used exactly once, program compiles cleanly.
module sigil;
cap type Fuel {}

actor Worker {
    init(f: Fuel) {}
    on Ping() -> i64 { return 0; }
}

entry actor Main {
    // `fuel` flows in as a linear message payload (a cap HELD in actor state is
    // borrow-only inside a handler — C010 — so `.split` on state fuel would be
    // rejected before this O001 point; the inexpressibility argument is about
    // `.split`'s consuming semantics, which a flowing param exhibits identically).
    on Tick(fuel: Fuel) -> i64 {
        let _w1 = spawn::<Worker>(fuel.split(1));
        let _w2 = spawn::<Worker>(fuel.split(1));  // MUTATION_SITE
        return 1;
    }
}
